From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 05:11:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] win32u, winex11: handle a display change the way the Windows
 kernel does.

Turning a phone running a remote desktop client from portrait to landscape
left Word in a frame the size of the old screen.  Three things were
different from Windows:

The desktop's process now owns the display configuration, as the kernel
does: it enumerates, commits and tells everybody.  A process that notices
the host has moved on asks the desktop to look again instead of publishing
what it saw itself -- several processes rewriting the configuration at once
is how one of them came to read a screen half written, and a process on
another X server published that server's screen for the whole session.

A configuration is committed in one step.  The enumeration is recorded and
replayed into the registry only once it has a monitor, so the moment a
remote session spends with no output while it swaps screens leaves the
stored configuration where it was, instead of every process falling back to
a stand-in monitor.  A host with a single mode keeps an emulated mode only
while the host stays in the mode it was set on.

After a real change -- and only then -- every top-level window is told with
WM_DISPLAYCHANGE, whoever changed the display, and each maximized window is
maximized again in its own thread, through WM_GETMINMAXINFO and with
SWP_FRAMECHANGED.  The window manager resizes the X window first, while the
configuration is still the old one, and Office works its client area out
from the monitor it has at that moment; nothing made it look again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit a8bf29779482613edbf59ac19f48feeb115d4d47)

